WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.410
No one knows how much coal, oil and gas
the world will burn between now and 2100.

2
00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.930
Yet climate models reach that far.

3
00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.830
They split the planet into grid cells,
typically 1 to 2 degrees across.

4
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:19.150
Tested on the past, the models' average
matches observed warming within 0.2 degrees

5
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.370
Celsius over most of
the historical period.

6
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:29.570
So how can anyone forecast 2100?

7
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:30.420
Extend the past trend in a straight line?

8
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.230
In this mission, you'll see how the
carbon cycle links emissions to warming,

9
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.250
and why melting ice makes it worse.

10
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.010
Then you'll test what models project
for global warming and sea-level rise,

11
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.410
and how far to trust them.

12
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.300
Your mission: explain how anyone
can forecast the climate of 2100.
